            <div class="t-century">6th Century · ~500 CE</div>
            <div class="t-event">Monastic Scriptoria</div>
            <div class="t-desc">Manuscripts produced almost exclusively in monasteries. Output minimal — each copy hand-scribed over weeks or months.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="t-century">9th Century · Carolingian Renaissance</div>
            <div class="t-event">First Significant Growth</div>
            <div class="t-desc">Charlemagne's court spurred literacy and manuscript copying. Palace schools created new demand for written texts.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="t-century">12th Century · Rise of Universities</div>
            <div class="t-event">Notable Expansion</div>
            <div class="t-desc">Bologna (1088), Oxford (1096), Paris (1150) drove demand. Commercial scriptoria emerged outside monasteries.</div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="t-century">14th Century · Elite Literacy</div>
            <div class="t-event">Secular Markets Emerge</div>
            <div class="t-desc">Wealthy merchants and nobles commission personal libraries. Vernacular literature grows alongside Latin scholarship.</div>
